<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dagobert of Pisa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">died 1105</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Daimbert of Pisa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dagobert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, also called Daimbert, was Archbishop of Pisa and the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Sent as papal legate, he led a Pisan fleet to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> and was elected patriarch at the end of 1099. His claims to authority over the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> brought conflict with King Baldwin I, who had him deposed; he died in 1105.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dagobert-of-pisa.jpg" alt="Dagobert of Pisa" width="320" height="340" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bohemond_daimbert.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
